00:46:17.340 --> 00:46:30.790
<v Ira Glass>How are we changed by prison? Breyten Breytenbach was incarcerated for seven years by the apartheid government of South Africa. He was caught reentering the country on his way to work with the political resistance during the apartheid regime. He's a painter and a writer.
<v Ira Glass>我们是如何被监狱改变的？布雷顿·布雷滕巴赫被南非种族隔离政府监禁了七年。他在重返该国准备在种族隔离政权下与政治抵抗组织合作时被捕。他是一名画家和作家。

00:46:30.790 --> 00:46:45.550
<v Ira Glass>In some ways, his time in prison was different from most American incarcerations. In one facility he was in, the prisoners sang each night from their cells. But in other ways, what happened to him is what's happened to many people, everywhere, over the centuries, when left in confinement.
<v Ira Glass>在某些方面，他的监狱时光与大多数美国人的监禁不同。在他待过的一个设施里，囚犯们每晚在牢房里唱歌。但在其他方面，发生在他身上的事情与几个世纪以来世界各地的人在被关押时发生的事情是一样的。

00:46:45.550 --> 00:47:27.650
<v Breyten Breytenbach>For the first two years in prison, I was kept in a very small area all by myself. So I literally had very little to see or to look at in terms of variety, in terms of what one had become used to. You know what I see normally is seeing people come and go, in different distances, and different colors, and different lights. That was all just one very gray soup. Never seeing further than, say, about six feet away from me. The cell was 13 feet by 6 feet. So that was the limit of one's field of vision. And there was no variety in that field of vision, either.
<v Breyten Breytenbach>在监狱的前两年，我一直独自待在一个非常小的区域里。所以实际上我几乎没什么可看、没什么可观察的，就多样性而言，就人们习惯的东西而言。你知道我通常看到的是人来人往，不同的距离，不同的颜色，不同的光线。那全是一碗灰色的浓汤。从未看过离我超过六英尺远的地方。牢房是13英尺乘6英尺。所以这就是一个人视野的极限。而且那个视野里也没有多样性。

00:47:27.650 --> 00:48:09.720
<v Breyten Breytenbach>And of course, one was suffering inevitably from vitamin deficiency. So that one's eyesight was going down all the time. And I hadn't been aware of it. In fact, I remember I became aware of this when they transported me from the Pretoria prison to the Cape Town prison, which took place in a closed truck. And it had an air vent that was slightly open. And I could peek through there and see the landscape going by. And I suddenly realized that my eyesight was shot, gone.
<v Breyten Breytenbach>当然，一个人不可避免地遭受维生素缺乏。所以一个人的视力一直在下降。我并没有意识到这一点。事实上，我记得当我被从比勒陀利亚监狱运送到开普敦监狱时，我意识到了这一点，这是在一辆封闭的卡车里进行的。它有一个稍微打开的通风口。我可以从那里偷看外面经过的风景。我突然意识到我的视力完了，没了。

00:48:09.720 --> 00:48:55.850
<v Breyten Breytenbach>The irredeemable or the unstoppable wearing away that time has on one in prison is a very strong awareness. You know that you are being changed without being able to measure the extent of the changes that are taking place. You know that that this is a process of degradation taking place. But since you have nothing against which to measure this, you can not fight it. And at the same time, you have no sense of time passing at all, because there's nothing to which to tie it. It's like water. It runs right through you.
<v Breyten Breytenbach>时间在监狱里对一个人造成的不可救药的或不可阻挡的磨损是一种非常强烈的意识。你知道你正在改变，却无法衡量正在发生的变化的程度。你知道这是一个正在发生的退化过程。但因为你没有任何东西来衡量它，你无法对抗它。同时，你根本没有时间流逝的感觉，因为没有任何东西可以把它关联起来。它就像水。它直接穿过你。

00:48:55.850 --> 00:49:17.960
<v Breyten Breytenbach>The one particular prison that I have in mind now, called Beverly Hills-- we called it Beverly Hills, in Pretoria. It's the hanging jail. It's where they execute people. Up to about 200 a year. Now, that has as its central characteristic the actual chamber, the room where people are executed.
<v Breyten Breytenbach>我现在想到那个特别的监狱，叫“比佛利山庄”——我们在比勒陀利亚叫它“比佛利山庄”。那是绞刑监狱。那是他们执行死刑的地方。每年多达200人。现在，它的核心特征是那个实际的刑室，执行死刑的房间。

00:49:17.960 --> 00:49:39.620
<v Breyten Breytenbach>But if you live in that prison, you never see it. You're intensely aware of it. You know it's there. You can hear it. You can hear the room, because you can hear the trapdoors opening. There is sort of this shuddering going through the building when people actually are being hanged in the morning. And then you hear the coffins being put together.
<v Breyten Breytenbach>但如果你住在那座监狱里，你永远看不到它。你强烈地意识到它的存在。你知道它在那里。你可以听到它。你可以听到那个房间，因为你可以听到活板门打开的声音。当人们早上真的被绞死时，建筑物里有一种颤抖。然后你可以听到棺材被组装起来的声音。

00:49:39.620 --> 00:50:14.150
<v Breyten Breytenbach>Hearing this room, or this enclosure, without actually seeing it, makes it a very mysterious heart of the place. So you really have the sense of being in a labyrinth. I think it's a real way in which the senses merge there. You literally see with your ears. Because I think that when you hear those sounds, that was your thread out of the labyrinth, in a sense.
<v Breyten Breytenbach>听到这个房间，或者这个封闭区，而实际上看不到它，使它成为这个地方非常神秘的心脏。所以你真的有一种身处迷宫的感觉。我认为那里的感官确实融合在一起了。你实际上是用耳朵看的。因为我认为当你听到那些声音时，在某种意义上，那是你走出迷宫的线索。

00:50:14.150 --> 00:50:37.880
<v Breyten Breytenbach>That was the way you learned about what other people were doing. The quality also of what they were involved in. Because you could sense, you could hear the quality of their voices change when they knew that they were going to be hanged in a week's time. It was definitely-- you could hear, when somebody sang that that person is going to die in a few days, as opposed to somebody else singing who probably still has two months or three months.
<v Breyten Breytenbach>那是你了解其他人正在做什么的方式。这也是他们所卷入事情的性质。因为你可以感觉到，你可以听到他们声音的变化，当他们知道自己一周内将被绞死时。它绝对是——你可以听到，当一个人唱歌时，那个人几天内就要死了，这与另一个可能还有两个月或三个月时间的人唱歌形成了对比。

00:50:37.880 --> 00:51:40.610
<v Breyten Breytenbach>And sometimes if it was only one person due to be hanged, he would sing all by himself, in the middle of the night. And then it would be very sad, because it would be much more of a melodic kind of a blues. The interesting thing, or the touching thing about that, is that when that happened, when one person sings all by himself in the middle of the night, due to be hanged in a day or two days' time, you can actually hear the quality of the listening of the other people. Because you know that everybody else in that prison is awake, lying there, listening to this [INAUDIBLE]. And everybody is giving him a chance to sing his song, as it were. You knew there would not be a single person who would be asleep then. You would actually hear people listening, lying with their ears close to the bars or to the walls.
<v Breyten Breytenbach>有时如果只有一个人要被绞死，他会在半夜独自唱歌。然后那会非常悲伤，因为它更像是一种旋律优美的蓝调。有趣的事情，或者说感人之处在于，当这种情况发生时，当一个人在半夜独自唱歌，一两天内将被绞死时，你实际上可以听到其他人倾听的品质。因为你知道那座监狱里的每个人都醒着，躺在那里，听着这个［听不清］。每个人都在给他唱歌的机会。你知道那时不会有一个人睡着。你实际上会听到人们倾听，耳朵贴在栏杆或墙壁上。

00:51:40.610 --> 00:52:26.700
<v Breyten Breytenbach>Desperately trying to communicate. Not because one wanted to alert people to the condition you were in, but you wanted to alert people to the fact that you were still alive. Because you doubted whether you were still alive. You suddenly realized, but life's going on outside there. Even the people who were the closest to you are continuing their lives. They must do that. You know, you went down the road to buy some cigarettes, and you just didn't come back again. And people wept and moaned for some time, but then life is such that we must go on. So they bowled around the absence and they go on. And they remember your birthday, perhaps, if [UNINTELLIGIBLE], or Christmas. And correspondingly, you stop existing, since in any event, you can not communicate.
<v Breyten Breytenbach>拼命地试图沟通。不是因为你想提醒人们你所处的状况，而是你想提醒人们你还活着。因为你怀疑自己是否还活着。你突然意识到，但外面的生活还在继续。即使是离你最近的人也在继续他们的生活。他们必须这样做。你知道，你出门去买点烟，结果就再没回来。人们哀悼了一阵，但生活就是这样，我们必须继续。所以他们绕过你的缺席，继续前进。他们会记得你的生日，也许，如果［听不清］，或者圣诞节。相应地，你停止存在，因为无论如何，你无法沟通。

00:52:26.700 --> 00:52:54.970
<v Breyten Breytenbach>That was a terrible feeling, of really feeling that one was dead. Particularly since you were cut off from the outside world. And you weren't aware what was happening outside. Then when messages did come in, you would experience them as messages from the other side of the grave, another life. Another life.
<v Breyten Breytenbach>那是一种可怕的感觉，真正感到自己已经死了。特别是由于你与外界隔绝。你不知道外面发生了什么。然后当消息传进来时，你会把它们当作来自坟墓另一端、另一个生命的消息。另一个生命。

00:52:54.970 --> 00:53:30.260
<v Breyten Breytenbach>I remember talking to friends in prison, saying, you know, will we meet again in 10 years' time. And we mustn't forget then what it was like now. The fear that you would lose some of the things you did or were taught in prison, or you learned in prison. We knew that, for instance, as you move away from the experience, the bad parts become obliterated. And you will loop back upon it sometimes even with some nostalgia. It was nice. We were all young together in prison.
<v Breyten Breytenbach>我记得在监狱里和朋友交谈，说，你知道，我们十年后还会见面吗？那时候我们绝不能忘记现在是什么样。担心你会失去你在监狱里做过的一些事情，或者被教导的东西，或者你在监狱里学到的东西。我们知道，例如，当你远离那种经历时，糟糕的部分会变得模糊。你会偶尔甚至带着一些怀旧之情回想它。那很好。我们在监狱里一起度过了年轻时光。

00:53:30.260 --> 00:53:53.060
<v Breyten Breytenbach>That's the way the human mind works, I think. It's always hiding. It always moves away from the tender, from the raw, which is nice, which is fine. That means one dies in glory, I'm sure, thinking that you've had a wonderful life and there has just been one big muck-up.
<v Breyten Breytenbach>我想这就是人类思维工作的方式。它总是隐藏。它总是从脆弱、原始的东西中走开，这很好，没问题。这意味着一个人死得光荣，我肯定，想着你有一个美妙的生活，只是发生了一次大混乱。

00:53:53.060 --> 00:54:28.870
<v Breyten Breytenbach>The colors in prison-- all the no-colors of public places. All sad public places, such as army camps and, I suppose, hospitals during the war years, and things like that. In other words, you see gray. You see metal colors. You see a kind of an off-green. With a bit of luck, you may see a bit of brown. But mostly it's infinite shades of gray and dirty green. We call it in French [SPEAKING FRENCH].
<v Breyten Breytenbach>监狱里的颜色——所有公共场所的“无色”。所有悲伤的公共场所，比如陆军营地，我想，战争年代的医院，诸如此类。换句话说，你看到灰色。你看到金属色。你看到一种灰绿色。幸运的话，你可能会看到一点棕色。但主要是无限的灰色和脏绿色。我们在法语里称之为［法语词汇］。

00:54:28.870 --> 00:55:46.460
<v Breyten Breytenbach>It's like if you deprive somebody of colors for a certain time, and then you introduce color, however small the area of color may be that you introduce, there will be an intense sort of a pang of recognition of that color. A real experience of that color. We live in a surfeit of colors every day. We no longer even notice. We're sitting, looking at pink roses on the wallpaper, you know? The white cover over a bed, or the darkness of a shirt, or whatever it is. These are so much part, we are washed over with the richness of colors all the time. But in a situation like that, when all of a sudden there's this eruption of a toffee wrapper, for instance, or a leaf that got blown over the wall, or even a thread that somehow got blown into the wall, a thread of material, blue, something like that, you can not possibly imagine the intense awareness experience of that color, as if you'd never seen color before.
<v Breyten Breytenbach>就像如果你在一段时间内剥夺某人的颜色，然后你引入颜色，无论你引入的颜色区域有多小，都会有一种强烈的认出那种颜色的阵痛。一种对那种颜色的真实体验。我们每天生活在颜色的过剩中。我们甚至不再注意了。我们坐着，看着墙纸上的粉红玫瑰，你知道吗？床上的白色床罩，或者衬衫的颜色，或者其他什么。这些都是我们的一部分，我们一直被丰富的颜色所冲刷。但在那样的情况下，当突然间出现了一个太妃糖包装纸，或者一片被吹过墙的叶子，甚至是一根不知怎么被吹进墙里的线，一种材料的线，蓝色的，类似那样的东西，你根本无法想象那种颜色的强烈意识体验，就好像你以前从没见过颜色一样。

00:55:46.460 --> 00:56:06.670
<v Breyten Breytenbach>It makes of you a very nervous, very tight person when you're out of prison. Because you're ultra-sensitive to sounds and colors and things like that. They become too much. There were too many colors when I came out. I couldn't take it all in at the same time.
<v Breyten Breytenbach>当你出狱时，这会使你成为一个非常紧张、非常紧绷的人。因为你对声音、颜色和诸如此类的事情超级敏感。它们变得太多了。我出来的时候颜色太多了。我无法同时接受所有这些。

00:56:06.670 --> 00:56:21.540
<v Breyten Breytenbach>And it also gave me a strange sense of superiority. I felt like I was moving into a world of zombies. Now, that is a complete turnabout. In prison, you know that you're living among zombies, because you yourself have become a zombie. I mean, that is what a long-term prison sentence does to you in due time.
<v Breyten Breytenbach>这也给我一种奇怪的优越感。我觉得我正在走进一个僵尸的世界。现在，那是一个完全的转变。在监狱里，你知道你生活在僵尸中间，因为你自己已经变成了僵尸。我的意思是，这就是长期监禁最终对你所做的事情。

00:56:21.540 --> 00:57:03.360
<v Breyten Breytenbach>And yet, when you come out you have a composite feeling of, you're moving among zombies. These people don't really know what life's about. They don't feel walking in the street. They don't see the colors. And they don't know what implications are. They don't feel the people coming up to them. They have no sense of that. They're not alive. Their antennae are not vibrating. And of course, particularly, they're not even aware of the moral implications of what it's like to be walking on the street, a free person. That puts you really out of step with the people whom you move among.
<v Breyten Breytenbach>然而，当你出来时，你有一种综合的感觉，你正在僵尸中间移动。这些人真的不知道生活是什么。他们在大街上走时没有任何感觉。他们看不见颜色。他们不知道暗示是什么。他们感觉不到走近他们的人。他们没有那种感觉。他们不活着。他们的天线没有在振动。当然，特别是，他们甚至没有意识到在大街上行走作为一个自由人是什么感觉的道德含义。这让你和你周围的人完全脱节。

00:57:03.360 --> 00:57:28.360
<v Ira Glass>Breyten Breytenbach was originally taped for a radio series called Territories of Art for the Museum of Contemporary Art in Los Angeles in 1986. He was recorded by Stephen Erickson and interviewed by Lawrence Weschler, who used the interviews in writing about Breytenbach for his book Calamities of Exile. Breytenbach himself recounts his experiences in his book, True Confessions of an Albino Terrorist.
<v Ira Glass>布雷顿·布雷滕巴赫最初于1986年为洛杉矶当代艺术博物馆的广播系列《艺术领土》录音。他由斯蒂芬·埃里克森录音，并由劳伦斯·韦施勒采访，韦施勒在他关于布雷滕巴赫的书《流亡的灾难》中使用了这些采访。布雷滕巴赫本人在他的书《白化恐怖分子的真实供词》中讲述了他的经历。

00:57:28.360 --> 00:57:40.090
<v Ira Glass>Well, our program was produced today by Nancy Updike and myself with Alix Spiegel, Julie Snyder, and Emi Takahara. Contributing editors Paul Tough, Jack Hitt, Margy Rochlin, and Consigliere Sarah Vowell. Production help from Todd Bachmann.
<v Ira Glass>好吧，我们的节目今天由南希·厄普代克和我，以及阿利克斯·斯皮格尔、朱莉·斯奈德和江美高原制作。贡献编辑保罗·塔夫、杰克·希特、玛吉·罗克林和顾问萨拉·沃威尔。托德·巴克曼提供制作帮助。

00:57:40.090 --> 00:57:55.750
